# 3. Crate Responsibilities
 
## 3.1 `ror-core`
 
Lowest-level semantic domain.
 
Contains:
 `Symbol ActorId CapRef EffectId EventSequence LogicalTime Expr Value FunctionValue Environment Constraint Effect EffectCost Budget Consumable Reserved Fault MachineEvent ` 
Also contains canonical semantic traits/interfaces where doing so does not create circular dependencies.
 
Must NOT contain:
 
 
- host calls;
 
- filesystem access;
 
- networking;
 
- scheduler implementation;
 
- persistence implementation;
 
- capability authority storage;
 
- LLM integration.
 

 
Dependency rule:
 `ror-core    ↓ standard library only ` 
Prefer no external dependencies unless an unavoidable frozen serialization/hash requirement is established.
  
# 4. `ror-compiler`
 
Responsible for converting untrusted language structures into validated executable plans.
 
Pipeline:
 `Block  ↓ parse  ↓ NormalizedAST  ↓ ValidatedPlan  ↓ PlanIR  ↓ capability analysis  ↓ resource analysis  ↓ ExecutablePlan ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-compiler/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── parser.rs     ├── normalize.rs     ├── validate.rs     ├── lower.rs     ├── capability_analysis.rs     ├── resource_analysis.rs     ├── plan.rs     └── error.rs ` 
Critical rule:
 `Block ≠ ExecutablePlan ` 
`ExecutablePlan` constructors must remain private to the compiler.
 
Production runtime must only receive an `ExecutablePlan`.
  
# 5. `ror-kernel`
 
Trusted authority/resource decision layer.
 
Contains:
 `CapabilityKernel AuthorityNode CapabilityContext capability derivation revocation authorization budget enforcement primitives logical-time validation ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-kernel/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── capability.rs     ├── authority.rs     ├── derivation.rs     ├── revocation.rs     ├── authorization.rs     ├── budget.rs     ├── resource.rs     └── error.rs ` 
Critical visibility rule:
 `pub(crate) struct AuthorityNode { ... } ` 
must remain inaccessible to evaluator/runtime consumers.
 
Public capability API exposes opaque handles:
 `pub struct CapRef {     ... } ` 
but not authority contents.
  
# 6. `ror-runtime`
 
Trusted execution machine.
 
Contains:
 `CEK evaluator continuations actors scheduler mailboxes effect issuance machine state runtime transitions ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-runtime/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── machine.rs     ├── evaluator/     │   ├── mod.rs     │   ├── state.rs     │   ├── frames.rs     │   ├── step.rs     │   ├── lambda.rs     │   ├── call.rs     │   ├── attenuate.rs     │   └── request.rs     │     ├── actor/     │   ├── mod.rs     │   ├── state.rs     │   ├── mailbox.rs     │   ├── spawn.rs     │   ├── send.rs     │   └── receive.rs     │     ├── scheduler.rs     ├── effect.rs     ├── transition.rs     └── fault.rs ` 
The evaluator may depend on kernel interfaces but must not gain authority introspection.
  
# 7. `ror-persistence`
 
Responsible for durable machine state.
 
Contains:
 `canonical persistence framing WAL snapshot effect journal checksums sequence validation recovery crash classification ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-persistence/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── frame.rs     ├── wal.rs     ├── snapshot.rs     ├── effect_journal.rs     ├── recovery.rs     ├── checksum.rs     ├── crash.rs     └── error.rs ` 
Persistence must reuse Phase 15A canonical semantic bytes.
 
It must not create an alternative semantic serialization.
  
# 8. `ror-host`
 
Host boundary.
 
Contains:
 `HostExecutor HostPolicy ReplayHost live host adapter interfaces effect request/receipt transport ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-host/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── executor.rs     ├── policy.rs     ├── replay.rs     ├── receipt.rs     └── error.rs ` 
The host cannot authorize an effect independently of the capability/resource gates.
 
The host is the final deployment boundary, not the semantic authority.
  
# 9. `ror-agent`
 
Higher-level autonomous-agent orchestration.
 
Contains:
 `Observation PlanProposal planner interface staleness validation accepted-plan recording supervisor runtime lifecycle ` 
Module structure:
 `ror-agent/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── observation.rs     ├── proposal.rs     ├── planner.rs     ├── acceptance.rs     └── supervisor.rs ` 
The planner interface is explicitly untrusted.
 `trait Planner {     fn propose(&mut self, observation: Observation)         -> PlanProposal; } ` 
The resulting proposal must pass the compiler.
  
# 10. `ror-reference`
 
Independent executable reference model.
 
This crate is intentionally separate from production runtime.
 
Structure:
 `ror-reference/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── model.rs     ├── value.rs     ├── environment.rs     ├── cek.rs     ├── capability.rs     ├── budget.rs     ├── actor.rs     ├── scheduler.rs     ├── effect.rs     ├── persistence.rs     ├── recovery.rs     └── observation.rs ` 
The reference model must NOT depend on:
 `ror-runtime ror-kernel ror-persistence ror-host ror-agent ` 
It may depend on the frozen primitive/domain definitions from `ror-core` only where doing so does not accidentally import production semantics.
 
Where semantic independence is important, define distinct reference types.
 
For example:
 `pub struct RefCapId(pub u64); pub struct RefActorId(pub u64); pub struct RefEffectId(pub u64); ` 
rather than reusing production identity objects.
  
# 11. `ror-differential`
 
The differential harness.
 
Structure:
 `ror-differential/ └── src/     ├── lib.rs     ├── generator.rs     ├── shrinker.rs     ├── runner.rs     ├── observation.rs     ├── comparator.rs     ├── counterexample.rs     ├── traces.rs     └── seed.rs ` 
Responsibilities:
 `generate  ↓ execute production  ↓ execute reference  ↓ normalize observations  ↓ compare  ↓ locate first divergence  ↓ shrink  ↓ persist counterexample ` 
The comparator is authoritative for differential verification.
  
# 12. `ror-testkit`
 
Shared testing infrastructure only.
 
Allowed contents:
