# 16. Visibility Rules
 
Default to private.
 
Use:
 `pub(crate) ` 
for trusted internal boundaries.
 
Use:
 `pub ` 
only when an API is intentionally part of the crate's external contract.
 
Examples:
 `pub struct CapRef { ... } ` 
may be public.
 
But:
 `pub struct AuthorityNode { ... } ` 
must NOT be public.
 
Likewise:
 `pub(crate) fn finalize(...) ` 
for executable-plan construction.
  
# 17. ExecutablePlan Construction Boundary
 
The runtime must never be able to construct an executable plan directly from arbitrary language data.
 
Preferred structure:
 `pub struct ExecutablePlan {     ir: PlanIR,     bounds: ResourceBounds,     _sealed: Sealed, } ` 
where construction is restricted to the compiler.
 
The runtime accepts:
 `fn execute(plan: &ExecutablePlan) ` 
not:
 `fn execute(block: &Block) ` 
This distinction is mandatory.
  
# 18. Capability Boundary
 
Production code must use:
 `CapRef ` 
rather than passing authority structures.
 
Authority storage remains internal to the kernel.
 
The runtime can request:
 `kernel.validate(...) kernel.authorize(...) kernel.derive(...) ` 
but cannot inspect:
 `scope parameters resource ceiling lifetime parent authority revocation state ` 
directly.
  
# 19. Host Boundary
 
No evaluator module may import:
 `std::fs std::net std::process ` 
or equivalent host-effect APIs.
 
External effects must flow through:
 `Expr::Request       ↓ Effect       ↓ Capability gate       ↓ Budget gate       ↓ Deadline gate       ↓ HostPolicy       ↓ Durable issuance       ↓ HostExecutor ` 
This is an architectural rule, not merely a coding convention.
  
# 20. Persistence Boundary
 
No runtime transition should directly perform arbitrary persistence I/O.
 
Use a persistence interface:
 `pub trait Persistence {     fn append(&mut self, record: WalRecord)         -> Result<(), PersistenceError>;      fn sync(&mut self)         -> Result<(), PersistenceError>; } ` 
The machine determines the semantic transition.
 
The persistence layer determines how that transition becomes durable.
  
# 21. Phase 15A Bootstrap
 
Before building the full runtime, implement canonical serialization.
 
Initial files:
 `ror-core/ └── src/     ├── canonical/     │   ├── mod.rs     │   ├── encode.rs     │   ├── decode.rs     │   ├── cursor.rs     │   ├── error.rs     │   └── tags.rs ` 
Implement:
 `CanonicalPayload CanonicalEncode CanonicalDecode ReadCursor envelope() checked_length() ` 
Required vectors:
 `Symbol(1) Value::Symbol(Symbol(1)) Map(Symbol(1) → false, Symbol(2) → true) ` 
Required rejection cases:
 `wrong version wrong type tag truncated header truncated payload length overflow invalid UTF-8 invalid discriminant invalid boolean trailing bytes duplicate map key `  
# 22. Phase 15A Golden Vector Policy
 
Golden vectors live outside implementation source:
 `vectors/canonical/ ` 
Each vector should include:
 `name semantic value canonical bytes expected digest where applicable ` 
Production and reference conformance tests consume the same vector corpus but use independent decoding logic.
  
# 23. Independent Reference Requirement
 
The reference model should be written early.
 
Do not postpone it until production is “complete.”
 
Preferred development sequence:
 `production component        ↓ reference component        ↓ differential test        ↓ mutation test        ↓ next component ` 
This minimizes the possibility that production implementation decisions silently become the specification.
  
# 24. Initial Production/Reference Pair
 
The first semantic milestone should be pure expressions.
 
Production:
 `Expr Environment Continuation CEK step ` 
Reference:
 `RefExpr RefEnvironment RefContinuation RefCEK step ` 
Test:
 `Production(expr)         ↕ Observation         ↕ Reference(expr) ` 
Start with:
 `Value Var Let Seq If ` 
Then add:
 `Lambda Call ` 
Then:
 `Attenuate Request ` 
Then actors.
 
Then persistence.
  
# 25. Test Organization
 
Unit tests stay close to their implementation.
 
Integration tests live under:
 `tests/ ` 
Recommended:
 `tests/conformance/ tests/exhaustive/ tests/property/ tests/mutation/ tests/crash/ tests/stress/ ` 
The separation is intentional.
 
A unit test answers:
 
 
Does this local component behave correctly?
 
 
A differential test answers:
 
 
Does production agree with the independent semantics?
 
 
A mutation test answers:
 
 
Can the verification system detect a known semantic defect?
 
 
A crash test answers:
 
 
Does durability/recovery preserve the frozen machine semantics?
 
  
# 26. Feature Flags
 
Avoid semantic feature flags.
 
Do not create:
 `feature = "legacy-call-order" feature = "unsafe-capabilities" feature = "old-budget" ` 
Frozen semantics should have one implementation.
 
Development-only instrumentation may use features, for example:
 `feature = "trace-debug" feature = "mutation" feature = "stress" ` 
but these must not alter normal semantic behavior.
  
# 27. Unsafe Rust Policy
 
Default policy:
 `#![forbid(unsafe_code)] ` 
at crate level unless a concrete implementation requirement demonstrates that `unsafe` is necessary.
 
Any future `unsafe` must have:
 `reason invariant safety proof tests review ` 
and must not weaken the capability/effect boundary.
 
The initial implementation should remain entirely safe Rust where practical.
  
# 28. Panic Policy
 
Trusted runtime code must not use panic as normal semantic control flow.
 
Recoverable conditions become explicit errors/faults.
 
Especially:
 `budget underflow invalid capability invalid receipt corrupt WAL sequence gap invalid snapshot marshal failure host failure ` 
must not be represented by accidental panics.
 
`unwrap()` and `expect()` should be restricted to statically justified invariants and reviewed individually.
  
# 29. Determinism Rules
 
Never depend on:
 `HashMap iteration order OS scheduling thread ID memory address wall-clock time random allocator behavior unordered host completion ` 
where semantic determinism matters.
 
Use deterministic structures such as:
 `BTreeMap BTreeSet VecDeque explicit IDs explicit logical time ordered traces ` 
Randomized testing is allowed.
 
Randomness must be an explicit test input, never hidden machine state.
  
# 30. Initial Error Taxonomy
 
Keep errors semantically distinct.
 
Actor-local:
 `ArityMismatch TypeError BudgetExhausted CapabilityDenied MarshalFault HostFault ReplayCorruption ` 
Global:
 `SnapshotCorruption EventLogCorruption EventSequenceGap InvalidRecoveredState EffectReconciliationFailure PersistenceFailure ActorTableCorruption SchedulerInvariantViolation ` 
Do not collapse all failures into a generic `RuntimeError`.
  
# 31. Obligation Registry
 
Create:
 `docs/verification/obligations.toml ` 
Each obligation has a stable identifier.
 
Example:
 `[[obligation]] id = "CEK-CALL-ARITY-PRECHECK" description = "Arity mismatch is detected before argument evaluation."  [[obligation]] id = "CEK-CALL-ARGS-LTR" description = "Arguments evaluate strictly left-to-right."  [[obligation]] id = "CAP-DERIVE-NO-AMPLIFICATION" description = "Derived authority cannot exceed parent authority."  [[obligation]] id = "BUDGET-ESCROW-CONSERVATION" description = "Effect completion escrow cannot be created or destroyed."  [[obligation]] id = "EFFECT-ISSUE-DURABLE-BEFORE-HOST" description = "Host invocation requires durable issuance."  [[obligation]] id = "RECOVERY-ISSUED-INDETERMINATE" description = "Issued but incomplete effects recover as Indeterminate unless reconciled."  [[obligation]] id = "WAL-GAP-REJECT" description = "WAL sequence gaps are rejected."  [[obligation]] id = "MARSHAL-CAPABILITY-REJECT" description = "Ordinary marshalling cannot transfer raw capabilities." ` 
Obligation IDs must remain stable.
  
# 32. Mutation Registry
 
Create:
 `mutations/registry.toml ` 
Each mutation contains:
 `id category description target expected_killed_by status ` 
Example:
 `[[mutation]] id = "M001" category = "evaluator" description = "Reverse argument evaluation order." target = "ror-runtime::evaluator::call" status = "active"  [[mutation]] id = "M002" category = "evaluator" description = "Skip arity precheck." target = "ror-runtime::evaluator::call" status = "active"  [[mutation]] id = "M003" category = "capability" description = "Accept revoked capability." target = "ror-kernel::capability" status = "active" ` 
Mutation IDs are never reused.
  
# 33. Counterexample Storage
 
Create:
 `artifacts/ ` 
locally and in CI.
 
A counterexample must contain:
 `seed generator_version semantic_version test_case_version minimized_program initial_state scheduler_trace host_trace crash_trace production_observation reference_observation first_divergence ` 
Do not rely on console output alone.
  
